 By Nathan Bomey &middot; Oct 29, 2026 
 
 
 Thursday ✅.

 Today's newsletter is 858 words, a 3-minute read. 

 📉 The dashboard: The S&amp;P 500 closed down 0.3%.

 ‼️ Stunning stat: Google parent Alphabet shed half a trillion in market value following yesterday's earnings. 🔥 Today's stock spotlight: Crocs (+25.5%) soared on record Q3 revenue driven by international demand for its namesake foam clogs and a surprise rebound in its HeyDude slip-on business. The results, combined with a strong showing from Adidas , breathed new life into other footwear-linked stocks, including Dick's Sporting Goods (+4.3%) and Nike (+3.45%).

 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 1 big thing: Cash-flow crater 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Brendan Lynch/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 It's as if Amazon said: Google, hold my beer.

 Amazon shares fell sharply in extended trading after it reported plummeting free cash flow tied to its heavy investments in AI. 💸 Zoom in: Free cash flow was negative $42.4 billion over the 12 months ended Sept. 30, compared with a positive $14.8 billion for the same time period a year ago.

 The company attributed the swing primarily to a $94.8 billion increase in purchases of property and equipment connected to its AI investments. It expects 2026 cash capital expenditures of $220 billion. 🔍 The big picture: Investors are scrutinizing the heavy costs of AI infrastructure investments — a microscope that also dragged down Alphabet shares today after the Google parent's earnings report last night.

 📉 Amazon shares are down around 6% in extended trading after already closing the day down 4.3%.

 Alphabet finished down 12.3%. Zoom out: Another data point from Amazon's earnings that might've unnerved investors was the 12% growth in advertising, compared with the same period a year earlier.

 That trailed the previous quarter's 26% year-over-year growth. The company's ad business could come under pressure as consumers turn to AI agents to help shop for them, though Amazon recently blocked Meta's popular Muse agent from its platform. "Muse traction will likely drive questions on Amazon's agentic strategy," Bank of America analyst Justin Post wrote this week in a research note. Go deeper 

 
 
