 2. Reddit ducks behind the curtain 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Illustration: Annelise Capossela/Axios

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Reddit is saying goodbye to the open web and moving primarily to a log-in model, saying it no longer wants its content subject to the prying eyes of AI web scrapers.

 Co-founder and CEO Steve Huffman told Axios' Sara Fischer today that the company is making the change to improve its user experience, bolster its ads business and prevent bots from scraping its proprietary data. He expressed confidence that the change would ultimately improve engagement and monetization, even as changes to traditional search continue to impact user growth in the U.S, Sara writes . What's next: Reddit will begin testing the fully logged-in experience this quarter outside the U.S. and collect data on the best approach before it rolls out broader changes gradually.

 The company is still working through what content will be available to users who do not log in. Participation features, such as commenting and content ranking, will continue to be accessible only to logged-in users. The big picture: The change was announced alongside Reddit's Q3 earnings results, which saw the ads business grow a whopping 54% year over year.

